{"gid":"2026_03_KC_MIA","week":3,"away":"KC","home":"MIA","away_col":"#FF4D57","home_col":"#2CD3C6","spread":-7.0,"total":44.5,"fav":"KC","hpts":19,"apts":26,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 27 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Script leans KC.</strong> As a 7-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile MIA throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Caleb Douglas</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>MIA</strong> welcome the <strong>KC</strong> as underdogs of <strong>7.0</strong>, with the total set at <strong>44.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>KC 26\u201319</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>38% of its snaps with KC in front</strong>, <strong>38% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with KC chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>KC should control</strong>. A favorite of 7 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>KC</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>MIA</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>MIA</strong> to run more total plays than KC \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for MIA's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p><strong>Patrick Mahomes</strong> adds rushing value \u2014 he scrambles on 4.8% of dropbacks, live for rush-yard and anytime-TD props.</p><p>Tough sledding for <strong>De'Von Achane</strong> \u2014 KC are stingy against the run (108 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p>Efficiency edge to KC \u2014 a 46% success-rate offense against a MIA defense that allows 46% \u2014 points should come.</p><p><strong>Travis Kelce</strong> has room to work \u2014 MIA allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Caleb Douglas</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 KC hold WR1s to just 50 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p>In the red zone KC throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Rashee Rice</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone MIA throw it (55% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Caleb Douglas</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A middling 44 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>KC \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>KC stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>50%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> (24 carries, 103 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though KC's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> projects for <strong>11 targets and 95 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Travis Kelce</strong> (TE, 9 targets, 70 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>MIA \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>MIA are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 MIA project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>De'Von Achane</strong>'s rushing projection (14 carries, 78 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Caleb Douglas</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, MIA funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 75 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 7-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"